<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sonoma, California</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sonoma is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> Bay, that grew around the Mission San Francisco Solano, the last of the California missions, founded in 1823. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-guadalupe-vallejo">Mariano Guadalupe Vallejo</a> laid out its plaza in 1835. In June 1846 the Bear Flag revolt began there with the seizure of the town, and it is now a centre of California's wine country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sonoma-california.jpg" alt="Sonoma, California" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cullen328 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sonoma_City_Hall_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the first Spaniards arrived, the area lay near the north-eastern corner of the territory of the Coast Miwok, with the Southern Pomo to the north-west, the Wappo to the north-east and the Suisun and Patwin peoples to the east. Mission San Francisco Solano, founded in 1823 by the priest José Altimira, Giovanni Toscano and Giovanni Álvarez under Governor Luis Antonio Argüello, was the only mission built by the Mexican authorities rather than the Spanish. It formed part of a plan to fortify the area north of San Francisco Bay against Russian expansion southward from Russian America.</p><p>After the Mexican secularisation act of 1833, Governor José Figueroa made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-guadalupe-vallejo">Mariano Guadalupe Vallejo</a> administrator of the mission and military commander of the Northern Frontier. In 1835, with the help of William A. Richardson, Vallejo laid out the streets, lots, central plaza and main avenue of the Pueblo de Sonoma on a grid prescribed by the Spanish Laws of the Indies. The Sonoma Barracks housed his troops. The pueblo remained under military control until a town council was established in 1844.</p><p>Before dawn on 14 June 1846 thirty-three Americans already in rebellion against the Mexican provincial government arrived in Sonoma, some of them from the camp of the army captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-c-fremont">John C. Frémont</a>. They met no resistance. They took Vallejo and three associates prisoner, though Vallejo had invited their leaders into his home to negotiate, and William Ide urged the rebels to stay and found a new republic. The remaining twenty-four made the Sonoma Barracks their headquarters and within days created their Bear Flag. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/californios">Californios</a> called them Los Osos, the Bears. The Bear Flag Revolt ended and the California Republic ceased on 9 July 1846, when Lieutenant Joseph Warren Revere of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> raised the American flag before the barracks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> became a state in 1850, Sonoma was the first seat of Sonoma County, but an election of 1854 moved the seat to Santa Rosa. Disputes over land titles from Vallejo's grants took some thirty years of lawsuits to settle. The City Hall in the middle of the Plaza was dedicated in 1908.</p><p>Sonoma is a city in Sonoma County in the North Bay region of the San Francisco Bay Area, one of the principal cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>'s wine country and the centre of the Sonoma Valley wine region. It had 10,739 residents in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The Plaza, laid out by Vallejo, remains the centre of town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sonoma, California” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Encouraged by the explorer John C. Frémont, about 30 of them seized the town of <b>Sonoma</b> on 14 June and took the local commander, Mariano Guadalupe Vallejo, prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/state/california-republic">California Republic</a></li></ul></section>
